// In-depth battles, batch c: kalinga, changping, trasimeno, canas, julu, zama.
// Checked against the English Wikipedia articles and the ancient sources they cite.

Object.assign(GLOSSARY, {
  "édito": "An official text issued by a ruler. Ashoka's edicts were carved on rocks and pillars all over India, and they are the oldest written documents of the country that we can still read.",
  "dharma": "An Indian word for law, duty and righteous conduct. Ashoka used it to speak of a government based on morality, tolerance and nonviolence.",
  "reinos combatentes": "A period of Chinese history, from about 475 to 221 BC, in which seven great kingdoms fought one another until Qin unified them all.",
  "besta": "A bow mounted on a wooden stock, with a trigger. It shot with force and accuracy without demanding years of training. The Chinese used it in mass centuries before Europe did.",
  "cerco": "Surrounding an army or a city and cutting off its exits and its supplies, until it surrenders or starves.",
  "cônsul": "One of the two magistrates elected every year to govern the Roman Republic and command its armies.",
  "legião": "The main unit of the Roman army, with about 4,000 to 5,000 heavy infantry soldiers, divided into small blocks called maniples.",
  "manípulo": "A block of about 120 Roman soldiers. Maniples stood in a checkerboard pattern, with gaps between them, which gave the legion flexibility.",
  "hastati": "The first line of the Roman legion: the youngest soldiers.",
  "principes": "The second line of the legion: men in their twenties and thirties, more experienced.",
  "triarii": "The third line of the legion: the veterans, who only went in when things got ugly.",
  "vélites": "Roman light infantry, young and poor men armed with javelins. They threw their javelins ahead of the legion and then fell back through the gaps between the maniples.",
  "númidas": "A people of North Africa, in present-day Algeria. Their light cavalry, riding without saddle or bridle, was the best in the Mediterranean.",
  "emboscada": "Hiding troops and attacking by surprise an enemy who is passing by without knowing that he is being waited for.",
  "ditador": "In Rome, a magistrate with total power, appointed only in emergencies and for six months at most. It did not have today's bad meaning.",
  "cerco duplo": "A maneuver in which the two ends of an army close in on the enemy from both sides at the same time, like a pincer. Also called double envelopment.",
  "fundibulários": "Soldiers who threw stones or lead bullets with a sling. Those from the Balearic Islands were the most famous of Antiquity.",
  "chengyu": "A Chinese expression of four characters, almost always taken from an ancient story. It works like a proverb."
});

Object.assign(DEEP, {
  kalinga: {
    war: "Conquest of Kalinga by the Mauryan Empire (c. 261 BC)",
    hook: "The only war whose winner carved an apology in stone.",
    chapters: [
      { n: "01", title: "The world before", blocks: [
        "Around 260 BC, the Mauryan Empire covered almost all of India, from present-day Afghanistan to Bengal. It had been founded by Chandragupta Maurya, Ashoka's grandfather, a few years after Alexander the Great reached the Indus River. The capital was Pataliputra, today's Patna, one of the largest cities in the world.",
        "On the east coast, in what is now Odisha and northern Andhra Pradesh, the kingdom of Kalinga was still independent. It was rich: it had ports, a fleet and trade by sea as far as Southeast Asia. And it lay right on the road between the Mauryan capital and southern India, which made it a strategic thorn.",
        { timeline: [
          ["321 BC", "Chandragupta overthrows the Nanda dynasty, which ruled over Kalinga, and founds the Mauryan Empire."],
          ["c. 268 BC", "Ashoka is crowned, after a dispute with his brothers over the succession."],
          ["c. 261 BC", "In the eighth year of his reign, Ashoka invades Kalinga."],
          ["Afterwards", "Ashoka has his edicts carved on rocks and pillars all over the empire."]
        ]}
      ]},
      { n: "02", title: "Main figures", blocks: [
        { h: "Ashoka, Mauryan emperor" },
        "Grandson of the founder of the empire, Ashoka came to the throne after a struggle for the succession. The later Buddhist tradition paints him, before Kalinga, as a cruel king, 'Ashoka the Fierce'. We should be wary: that tradition had an interest in showing a dramatic conversion. What he wanted was clear, and it was what every emperor wanted: to complete the empire.",
        { h: "Kalinga" },
        "From the defeated side, not a single name survives. We do not know who the king or chief of Kalinga was, or how the kingdom was governed. The Greek Megasthenes, who lived at the court of Chandragupta, described the army of Kalinga as having infantry, cavalry and elephants; the Roman Pliny, quoting him, speaks of 60,000 foot soldiers, 1,000 horsemen and 700 elephants, figures from a generation before the war."
      ]},
      { n: "03", title: "Step by step", blocks: [
        { h: "What we know, and what we do not" },
        "Almost everything we know about the war comes from a single source: the winner himself. In rock [[edict|édito]] number 13, Ashoka says that 150,000 people were carried off from Kalinga, 100,000 were killed and many times that number died of other causes, such as famine and disease.",
        { note: "There is no account of the battle itself: no size of the armies, no terrain, no movements. Local tradition places the fighting near the hills of Dhauli, by the Daya River, near Bhubaneswar, but this is a guess. That is why this page has no diagram." },
        "The numbers in the edict should be read with care. They are huge for the time, and they were written by someone who wanted to show how great his remorse was. Even so, it is rare in history for a winner to tell of the damage he did instead of hiding it.",
        { h: "The remorse" },
        "The same edict says that, after conquering Kalinga, the emperor felt deep remorse, and that the death, the deportation and the suffering of ordinary people, including the religious and the innocent, weighed heavily on him. From then on, says Ashoka, the only conquest that mattered was that of the [[dharma]]: righteous conduct."
      ]},
      { n: "04", title: "Legacy", blocks: [
        { h: "Result" },
        "Kalinga was annexed to the empire. Ashoka never again waged wars of conquest, and decades of relative peace followed. The edicts scattered across the empire preach religious tolerance, respect for parents and elders, the protection of animals and moderation.",
        { h: "Why it matters" },
        "Ashoka sponsored Buddhism and sent missionaries outside India, including to Sri Lanka, from where the religion spread through Southeast Asia. The lion capital of the Sarnath pillar is today the official emblem of India, and the wheel of the dharma sits at the center of the Indian flag.",
        { h: "Curiosities and controversies" },
        { note: "The conversion: the story that Ashoka became a Buddhist because of Kalinga is the popular version. There are signs that he was already drawing close to Buddhism before the war, and the change may have been more gradual." },
        { note: "The date: most authors say about 261 BC, but some prefer about 265 BC. Mauryan dates depend on indirect calculations." },
        { note: "Edict 13 was not carved in Kalinga itself. There, at Dhauli and Jaugada, Ashoka placed special edicts, which speak of governing the conquered people well and do not mention the massacre." }
      ]}
    ]
  },

  changping: {
    war: "War between Qin and Zhao, Warring States period (262–260 BC)",
    hook: "Two years of trenches, 46 days of hunger and the general who knew war only from books.",
    chapters: [
      { n: "01", title: "The world before", blocks: [
        "In the 3rd century BC, China was not a country. It was a board of seven kingdoms that had been fighting one another for more than 200 years: the period of the [[Warring States|reinos combatentes]]. The strongest was Qin, in the west, with an organized army, harsh laws and peasants who earned titles of nobility for the enemy heads they brought in. The main rival in the north was Zhao, famous for its cavalry.",
        "The spark was Shangdang, a mountainous region of the kingdom of Han. In 265 BC, Qin took the city of Qinyang and cut Shangdang off from the rest of Han. The local governor, Feng Ting, preferred to hand the region over to Zhao rather than let it fall into the hands of Qin. King Xiaocheng of Zhao accepted. It was a poisoned gift: it meant war with Qin.",
        { timeline: [
          ["265 BC", "Qin isolates Shangdang from the rest of the kingdom of Han."],
          ["262 BC", "Feng Ting hands Shangdang over to Zhao. Qin invades, under General Wang He."],
          ["262–260 BC", "The veteran Lian Po holds Qin back in fortified lines behind the Dan River."],
          ["260 BC", "Zhao replaces Lian Po with Zhao Kuo. Qin secretly replaces Wang He with Bai Qi."],
          ["September 260 BC", "After 46 days surrounded, the army of Zhao surrenders."]
        ]}
      ]},
      { n: "02", title: "Main figures", blocks: [
        { h: "Lian Po, the old general of Zhao" },
        "One of the most respected generals of his time. After losing the first positions, he understood that Qin won in open country and that Zhao's advantage was another one: Qin brought its food over difficult mountains, from very far away. So he dug fortified lines and refused to come out. It was the right strategy, and the court of Zhao, impatient and short of money, could not bear to wait.",
        { h: "Zhao Kuo, the scholar" },
        "Son of the great general Zhao She, he knew the treatises on war by heart and won any debate on strategy, even against his father. According to the historian Sima Qian, his own father said that he talked about war as if it were easy, and his mother asked the king not to appoint him. The king appointed him anyway. Qin helped, spreading rumors that Lian Po was old and cowardly, and that only Zhao Kuo frightened the generals of Qin.",
        { h: "Bai Qi, the butcher of Qin" },
        "The most feared general of the age, a specialist in battles of annihilation. Qin put him in command in secret: whoever revealed the change would be executed. He wanted Zhao to attack, believing it was facing the cautious Wang He."
      ]},
      { n: "03", title: "Step by step", blocks: [
        { h: "The forces" },
        "According to Sima Qian, the army of Zhao had more than 450,000 men, and the two sides together came close to a million. These are figures from the chronicles, written about 150 years later, and almost certainly exaggerated. Even discounting them, it was one of the largest battles of the ancient world.",
        { note: "Practically everything we know comes from the 'Records of the Grand Historian' (Shiji), by Sima Qian. The maneuver details below follow the traditional reconstruction, based on him." },
        { h: "The terrain" },
        "A valley between mountains in the southeast of the present-day province of Shanxi, near Gaoping, cut by the Dan River. Zhao was entrenched on the east bank; Qin, on the west. Zhao's food depots lay behind it, in a camp to the south.",
        { formation: true },
        { h: "The bait" },
        "Zhao Kuo arrived in July 260 BC and immediately abandoned the defensive strategy. He crossed the Dan River and attacked the camp of Qin. Bai Qi had prepared the ground: he left the river front weak and fortified the hills behind it. The troops of Qin fell back, as if they were losing, and Zhao followed, farther and farther from its own lines.",
        { h: "The trap closes" },
        "Meanwhile, two forces of Qin were moving around the outside. One, of about 25,000 men, crossed the mountains to the north and cut Zhao's link to its rear. Another, of 5,000 horsemen, cut the road to the depots in the south. The attack of Zhao hit the fortifications on the hills and stopped. When Zhao Kuo wanted to go back, the way was closed.",
        "It was a [[siege|cerco]] in the proper sense. Chinese historians compare it to Cannae, which would happen 44 years later on the other side of the world. The difference is time: at Cannae it lasted an afternoon; at Changping, weeks.",
        { h: "The 46 days" },
        "Zhao Kuo entrenched himself on a hill and waited for help. It did not come. The other kingdoms, which Zhao had irritated with its changes of alliance, stayed out. On the side of Qin, King Zhaoxiang went in person to the neighboring region of Henei, gave titles to everyone and drafted every man over 15 to close the ring.",
        "For 46 days, Qin attacked and Zhao resisted. The food ran out, then the horses. The chronicles say that the soldiers even ate one another. In the end, Zhao Kuo gathered his best troops to try to break the encirclement and died, hit by the archers and the [[crossbow|besta]] shooters of Qin. The rest of the army surrendered."
      ]},
      { n: "04", title: "Legacy", blocks: [
        { h: "The massacre" },
        "Bai Qi had too many prisoners to feed and too many to release. According to Sima Qian, he had them all killed, except for 240 of the youngest, who were sent back to Zhao to tell what they had seen. The chronicles add up 450,000 dead from Zhao, counting the battle and the execution, and say that Qin also lost half of its soldiers.",
        { note: "Tradition says that the prisoners were buried alive. The Chinese word used may mean only 'bury in a pit', and the numbers are almost certainly exaggerated. But the massacre was real: pits with thousands of skeletons are still found at the site." },
        { h: "Consequences" },
        "Bai Qi wanted to march straight to Handan, the capital of Zhao. The king preferred to give the troops a rest. When the attack came, Zhao had obtained help from Chu and Wei, and Qin suffered a great defeat at Handan. Bai Qi, who refused to command the new campaign, was forced by the king to kill himself.",
        "Zhao never recovered the men it lost. Qin needed a few years, but it came back stronger and, in 221 BC, unified China. The first emperor, Qin Shi Huang, is the one of the terracotta army, and the word 'China' probably comes from 'Qin'.",
        { h: "Curiosities and controversies" },
        { note: "Zhao Kuo gave rise to a [[chengyu]]: 'talking about war on paper' (zhi shang tan bing), for someone who masters theory and fails in practice." },
        { note: "In the 8th century, the Tang emperor Xuanzong had a temple built over bones found on the battlefield." }
      ]}
    ]
  },

  trasimeno: {
    war: "Second Punic War (218–201 BC)",
    hook: "An entire army walked into the fog and almost no one came out.",
    chapters: [
      { n: "01", title: "The world before", blocks: [
        "Rome and Carthage had been fighting over the western Mediterranean since the First Punic War, which Carthage lost in 241 BC. In Hispania, the Barca family built a new Carthaginian empire, with silver and soldiers. In 219 BC, Hannibal Barca attacked Saguntum, a city allied to Rome, and began the second war.",
        "Instead of waiting for Rome in Hispania, Hannibal took the war to Italy. He crossed the Alps in the autumn of 218 BC and arrived with about 20,000 foot soldiers, 6,000 horsemen and a few elephants. The Gauls of northern Italy, who hated Rome, joined him.",
        { timeline: [
          ["219 BC", "Hannibal takes Saguntum. Rome declares war."],
          ["October 218 BC", "Hannibal crosses the Alps."],
          ["November 218 BC", "He wins a cavalry battle at the Ticinus River."],
          ["December 218 BC", "He destroys a Roman army at the Trebia River."],
          ["Spring 217 BC", "He crosses the Apennines and the marshes of the Arno and plunders Etruria."],
          ["21 June 217 BC", "Ambush at Lake Trasimene."]
        ]},
        "The crossing of the Arno marshes, in spring, was terrible. According to Livy, the men walked for days with water up to their waists, many animals died and Hannibal himself, sick, lost the sight of one eye."
      ]},
      { n: "02", title: "Main figures", blocks: [
        { h: "Hannibal Barca, about 30 years old" },
        "Son of Hamilcar Barca, who according to tradition made him swear eternal hatred of Rome when he was a child. He had already won twice in Italy. His plan was not to take Rome, but to defeat its armies until the Italian allies abandoned the Republic. For that, he needed big, fast victories.",
        { h: "Gaius Flaminius, consul of Rome" },
        "A popular politician of modest origin, who had already defeated the Gauls and built the Via Flaminia. The aristocracy did not like him, and the ancient sources, written from its point of view, paint him as impetuous and disrespectful toward the gods. With his army at Arretium, he saw Hannibal pass by his side and burn the farms of Etruria. He set off after him in a hurry, without waiting for the other [[consul|cônsul]], Gnaeus Servilius Geminus, who was coming from Ariminum."
      ]},
      { n: "03", title: "Step by step", blocks: [
        { h: "The forces" },
        "Flaminius had four legions, two Roman and two of allies: about 25,000 men, perhaps a little more. Hannibal had quite a few more, the usual estimate being about 50,000, with Africans, Hispanics, Gauls and [[slingers|fundibulários]] from the Balearic Islands.",
        { h: "The terrain" },
        "The road ran along the northern shore of Lake Trasimene, in Umbria. To the north, low hills. The farther east, the closer the hills came to the water, until they left only a narrow strip and a gorge at the exit. It was a corridor with a wall on one side and the lake on the other.",
        { note: "The lakeshore has changed a lot since Antiquity, and the exact layout of the valley is debated. The order of the troops below follows Polybius and the most accepted reconstruction." },
        { h: "The trap" },
        { formation: true },
        "On 20 June, Hannibal camped in plain sight, at the point where the hills touched the lake, by the exit of the valley. At night, he took the rest of the army behind the hills and hid it on the slopes that the Romans could not see. The cavalry stayed farther west, near the entrance of the valley, to close it. In the middle, the Gauls. To the east, near the camp, the best infantry, Africans and Hispanics. The light infantry was in the gorge or reinforcing the center; historians disagree.",
        "Flaminius reached the edge of the lake at the end of the day and camped at the entrance. He did not send horsemen to scout the hills.",
        { h: "The morning of 21 June" },
        "The Romans set out at dawn and entered the corridor in column of march, probably with a screen of light infantry in front. A fog rose from the lake and covered the valley; the hills, higher up, stood above it. The Carthaginians saw the Romans; the Romans saw nothing.",
        "When the head of the column bumped into the Africans, near the exit, Hannibal gave the signal. All the troops came down the hills at the same time. The Romans heard shouts and trumpets to the left, behind and in front, without knowing where the attack was coming from. There was no time to form a battle line: the Roman [[legion|legião]] was excellent head-on, but this was an [[ambush|emboscada]] from the side, against a single file.",
        "The fighting lasted about three hours. The Gauls, in the center, suffered most of the Carthaginian losses, but they crushed the Romans group by group. According to Livy, the fury was such that nobody felt an earthquake that brought down cities in the region that day.",
        { h: "The death of Flaminius" },
        "Polybius says that Flaminius was lost and could not command. Livy gives him a more dignified death: he tried to rally the men and was killed by a Gallic horseman named Ducarius, who recognized him.",
        { h: "The lake" },
        "Trapped, many Romans went into the lake, trying to swim. In armor, they drowned. Others stood with water up to their necks, and the Carthaginian cavalry rode into the water to kill them.",
        { h: "The 6,000 at the front" },
        "About 6,000 Romans from the head of the column broke through the gorge, almost without resistance, and climbed a hill. When the fog lifted, they saw the size of the disaster. They were surrounded that same day by the cavalry of Maharbal and surrendered on the promise of leaving free. Hannibal freed the Italian allies and sold the Romans as slaves."
      ]},
      { n: "04", title: "Legacy", blocks: [
        { h: "Result" },
        "About 15,000 Romans died, according to Polybius and Fabius Pictor, a Roman senator of the time. Almost all the others were captured. Hannibal lost about 1,500 men, according to Polybius, or 2,500, according to Livy, most of them Gauls.",
        "A few days later, the cavalry of the other consul, some 4,000 men who were coming to reinforce Flaminius knowing nothing, was also destroyed. In two weeks, Rome had lost an entire army and the cavalry of another.",
        { h: "Consequences" },
        "In Rome, a magistrate announced in the Forum: 'We have been defeated in a great battle'. Rome appointed a [[dictator|ditador]], Quintus Fabius Maximus, who invented the strategy of refusing battle and wearing Hannibal down. Because he was nicknamed 'the Delayer', he gave his name to the word 'Fabian'. The patience did not last long: a year later, Rome decided to attack again, at Cannae.",
        { h: "Why it matters" },
        "The military historian Liddell Hart called it 'the greatest ambush in history'. It is perhaps the only case of a whole large army destroyed in an ambush.",
        { h: "Curiosities and controversies" },
        { note: "The names of the place: villages in the region are called Ossaia, from 'bones', and Sanguineto, from 'blood'. Local tradition links the names to the battle, but the link is not proven." },
        { note: "The fog: it is in the ancient authors, but some modern historians think it was exaggerated to explain the defeat." }
      ]}
    ]
  },

  canas: {
    war: "Second Punic War (218–201 BC)",
    hook: "The afternoon the greatest army of Rome was shut in a sack and destroyed.",
    chapters: [
      { n: "01", title: "The world before", blocks: [
        "After Trasimene, Rome changed tactics. The [[dictator|ditador]] Fabius Maximus followed Hannibal closely, never accepting battle, and let him plunder Italy. It worked, but it was humiliating: the Romans watched the farms of their allies burning and the army standing still. At the end of 217 BC, Fabius left office, and in Rome the idea gained strength of finishing off Hannibal once and for all, with the largest possible army.",
        "For 216 BC, the consuls Gaius Terentius Varro and Lucius Aemilius Paullus were elected. According to Polybius, the Senate put eight legions in the field, something Rome had never done, and with the allies the army passed 80,000 men.",
        { timeline: [
          ["June 217 BC", "Trasimene. Fabius Maximus is appointed dictator."],
          ["End of 217 BC", "Fabius leaves office. The strategy of waiting loses support."],
          ["Early 216 BC", "Varro and Paullus are elected consuls and receive the largest army in the history of Rome."],
          ["Summer 216 BC", "Hannibal takes the depot of Cannae, in Apulia, and cuts the Roman supplies."],
          ["2 August 216 BC", "The battle, by the Aufidus River."]
        ]}
      ]},
      { n: "02", title: "Main figures", blocks: [
        { h: "Hannibal Barca, 31 years old" },
        "He had won three times in Italy, but no important ally of Rome had changed sides. He needed a victory that nobody could ignore. He chose the ground, the hour and even the direction of the sun. According to Plutarch, when the officer Gisco remarked in alarm on the size of the Roman army, Hannibal answered that there was something more astonishing: among all those men, not one was called Gisco. The whole army laughed.",
        { h: "Varro and Aemilius Paullus" },
        "Since the two consular armies were together, the law ordered the consuls to alternate in command, one day each. The traditional version says that Varro, impetuous and of plebeian origin, was in command on the day of the battle and forced the fight, against the opinion of the prudent Paullus, an aristocrat. Modern historians are suspicious: Paullus's descendants were powerful and were protectors of Polybius, and Varro may have served as a scapegoat. Paullus died in the battle; Varro escaped.",
        { h: "The other names" },
        "On the Carthaginian side, Mago, Hannibal's brother, stayed with him in the center. Hasdrubal commanded the heavy cavalry on the left, and Hanno, the [[Numidians|númidas]] on the right. On the Roman side, Servilius Geminus, consul of the previous year, commanded the center; Paullus, the cavalry on the right; Varro, the one on the left."
      ]},
      { n: "03", title: "Step by step", blocks: [
        { h: "The forces" },
        "Rome had about 86,000 men: some 55,000 heavy infantry, about 15,000 [[velites|vélites]] and 6,400 horsemen. Of these, about 10,000 stayed to guard the camps and did not fight. Hannibal had some 50,000: 32,000 heavy infantry (21,000 Gauls, 3,000 Hispanics and 8,000 Africans), 8,000 light infantry, including 2,000 Balearic [[slingers|fundibulários]], and 10,000 horsemen. He had fewer men, but the better cavalry.",
        { note: "The numbers are from Polybius, the most reliable of the ancient authors. Livy and others give different totals, and there are modern historians who think the Roman army was smaller." },
        { h: "The terrain and the sun" },
        "A plain by the Aufidus River, today's Ofanto, in Apulia. The Romans had the river on their right, which protected their flank but also squeezed them. According to Livy, the southeast wind blew dust into their faces.",
        { h: "The Roman plan" },
        "The Romans knew they did not have good cavalry. They bet everything on the infantry: instead of the usual wide line, they put the [[maniples|manípulo]] closer together and much deeper. The idea was a steamroller that would punch through Hannibal's center by brute force, as they had almost managed at the Trebia. It was exactly what Hannibal expected.",
        { h: "Hannibal's plan" },
        { formation: true },
        "Hannibal put the Gauls and Hispanics, the least reliable infantry, in the center, and advanced the center in an arc, with its belly facing the Romans. At the ends of the line, set back, stood the Africans, the best infantry, armed in part with Roman equipment taken at Trasimene. He and Mago stayed in the center, where the pressure would be greatest.",
        { h: "First: the cavalries" },
        "By the river, the heavy cavalry of Hasdrubal, 6,000 to 7,000 Hispanics and Gauls, faced the Roman cavalry of Paullus. The space was so tight that many horsemen dismounted and fought on foot. The Romans were destroyed. Hasdrubal did not waste time pursuing: he passed behind the Roman army and went to the other end.",
        "At the other end, the Numidians of Hanno only held the allied cavalry of Varro, with skirmishing. When Hasdrubal arrived from behind, the allied cavalry fled. Hasdrubal left the pursuit to the Numidians and turned back.",
        { h: "Second: the center that gives way" },
        "Meanwhile, the infantries met. The legions hit the Gallic and Hispanic arc, which fell back step by step, without breaking. The arc straightened out, then bent inward. The Romans felt they were winning and pushed harder and harder, and the rear lines squeezed forward to join the fight.",
        { h: "Third: the pincer" },
        "Now the Roman mass was inside a sack, with the Africans standing still on both sides, perhaps hidden by the dust. Hannibal gave the order: the Africans turned inward and attacked both Roman flanks at the same time. It was the [[double envelopment|cerco duplo]].",
        { h: "Fourth: the door shuts" },
        "The cavalry of Hasdrubal came back and attacked the rear of the Roman infantry. The army was surrounded on all sides. The men inside were so tightly packed that they could not raise their weapons. The rest of the afternoon was a slaughter. Aemilius Paullus was wounded and died on the field. Varro fled with about 70 horsemen to Venusia."
      ]},
      { n: "04", title: "Legacy", blocks: [
        { h: "Result" },
        "Polybius speaks of 70,000 Romans dead and 10,000 captured. Livy, of 48,200 dead. Paullus, Servilius Geminus, 29 of the 48 military tribunes and about 80 senators, or men who could have been, died. Hannibal lost 5,700 men, according to Polybius, most of them Gauls from the center, or 8,000, according to Livy.",
        { h: "Knowing how to win, not how to use it" },
        "According to Livy, Maharbal, the commander of the Numidian cavalry, asked to march on Rome at once. Hannibal refused, and Maharbal is said to have answered: 'You know how to win, Hannibal, but you do not know how to use a victory'. Historians still argue about it. Hannibal had no siege engines, and Rome had walls and plenty of people. His bet was another one: the allies.",
        { h: "What came next" },
        "The bet worked in part. Capua, the second city of Italy, changed sides, and much of the south too. Philip V of Macedon and Syracuse allied themselves with Carthage. But Rome did not negotiate. The Senate refused to receive Hannibal's envoys, enlisted slaves and prisoners and returned to the strategy of Fabius. The war lasted 14 more years, and ended at Zama, with the survivors of Cannae among the winners.",
        { h: "Why it matters" },
        "Cannae became the model of the battle of annihilation. The German general Schlieffen based on it the plan for the invasion of France in 1914, and the American Schwarzkopf said he had thought of it in the Gulf War, in 1991.",
        { h: "Curiosities and controversies" },
        { note: "The date: the ancient source says 2 August, but the Roman calendar was out of place. In our calendar, it may have been early July." },
        { note: "The trick of the 500: Livy and Appian tell that 500 Numidians pretended to surrender and then attacked the Romans from inside. Many historians doubt the story." },
        { note: "The rings: according to Livy, Mago took to Carthage the gold rings of the dead Roman knights and poured them out on the floor of the Senate. There were said to be so many that they were measured in bushels." }
      ]}
    ]
  },

  julu: {
    war: "Fall of the Qin dynasty (209–206 BC)",
    hook: "He sank his own boats and broke the cooking pots: either they won, or they starved.",
    chapters: [
      { n: "01", title: "The world before", blocks: [
        "In 221 BC, Qin had unified China after Changping and other wars. The first emperor ruled with very harsh laws, forced labor and giant projects, such as the Great Wall and his mausoleum. When he died, in 210 BC, the empire began to crumble.",
        "In 209 BC, two soldiers who were late and would be executed for the delay rebelled: Chen Sheng and Wu Guang. The revolt caught fire all over the country, and the old kingdoms destroyed by Qin, such as Chu and Zhao, were reborn. The Qin general Zhang Han reacted well and crushed one rebel after another.",
        { timeline: [
          ["210 BC", "The first emperor of Qin dies."],
          ["209 BC", "Revolt of Chen Sheng and Wu Guang."],
          ["208 BC", "Zhang Han defeats Xiang Liang, leader of Chu, at Dingtao. Xiang Liang dies."],
          ["208 BC", "Zhang Han takes Handan and has the king of Zhao besieged at Julu."],
          ["Winter 208–207 BC", "Xiang Yu kills the commander Song Yi, takes over the army of Chu and crosses the river."],
          ["207 BC", "Battle of Julu. Months later, Zhang Han surrenders."]
        ]}
      ]},
      { n: "02", title: "Main figures", blocks: [
        { h: "Xiang Yu, about 25 years old" },
        "Nephew of Xiang Liang and grandson of a Chu general killed fighting Qin. Sima Qian describes him as huge, strong enough to lift a bronze cauldron, and unable to accept being second. The king of Chu sent the army to relieve Julu under the command of Song Yi, with Xiang Yu as deputy. Song Yi stopped at Anyang and waited 46 days. On the 47th, Xiang Yu entered his tent, killed him and took command, accusing him of treason. The king approved afterwards.",
        { h: "Zhang Han" },
        "The best Qin general at the end of the dynasty. He had armed convicts and slaves to face the rebels and had beaten almost all of them. But he served a court dominated by the eunuch Zhao Gao, who saw enemies everywhere."
      ]},
      { n: "03", title: "Step by step", blocks: [
        { h: "The forces" },
        "The chronicles give Xiang Yu about 70,000 men, and speak of hundreds of thousands on the side of Qin: the army of Wang Li surrounding Julu and that of Zhang Han farther back, protecting the supply road. These are figures from Sima Qian and probably exaggerated. There were also other rebel armies camped nearby, which did not want to take a risk.",
        { note: "Almost everything comes from the 'Records of the Grand Historian', by Sima Qian, written about a hundred years later. The movements are described in a few lines, and that is why this page has no diagram." },
        { h: "The crossing" },
        "Xiang Yu first sent Ying Bu and Zhongli Mo with 20,000 men to cross the river and attack the road that carried food to the army of Wang Li. They won a few skirmishes. Then he crossed with the whole army.",
        { h: "Breaking the pots, sinking the boats" },
        "On the other side, he gave a famous order: sink the boats, break the cooking pots and burn the tents, and give each soldier food for three days. There was no way to go back and no way to wait. Either they won, or they died.",
        { h: "The nine battles" },
        "The men of Chu attacked the army surrounding Julu nine times, cut its supply lines and ended up dismantling it. Sima Qian says that each Chu soldier was worth ten. The general Su Jiao died, Wang Li was captured and She Jian killed himself, burning himself, rather than surrender.",
        "Meanwhile, the armies of the other rebels watched from the top of their palisades, without moving. When it was all over and Xiang Yu summoned the commanders, they came in on their knees and did not dare lift their eyes. From that day on, he was the chief of all the rebels."
      ]},
      { n: "04", title: "Legacy", blocks: [
        { h: "The surrender of Zhang Han" },
        "Zhang Han fell back and sent a request for reinforcements to the capital. Zhao Gao accused him of failure and treason, and the reinforcements did not come. Trapped between Xiang Yu and his own court, Zhang Han surrendered in the summer of 207 BC, with about 200,000 men.",
        { h: "The massacre of Xin'an" },
        "A few months later, at Xin'an, Xiang Yu feared a mutiny among the surrendered soldiers and had all 200,000 killed, according to Sima Qian, buried alive. He spared only Zhang Han and two officers, whom he made kings in the old heartland of Qin. In 1912, workers building a railway at Yima, near the site, found large quantities of human bones.",
        { h: "Consequences" },
        "Without an army, the Qin dynasty fell: Liu Bang entered the capital and received the surrender of the last ruler at the end of 207 BC. Xiang Yu arrived later, had him executed and burned the palace. The two allies became rivals, and in 202 BC Liu Bang won and founded the Han dynasty, which gave its name to the Chinese people to this day.",
        { h: "Curiosities" },
        { note: "Julu gave the Chinese language at least three [[chengyu]]: 'break the pots and sink the boats', for a decision with no way back; 'one against ten'; and 'sit on the wall and watch', for someone who sits on the fence." }
      ]}
    ]
  },

  zama: {
    war: "Second Punic War (218–201 BC)",
    hook: "Hannibal against the only Roman who had learned from him.",
    chapters: [
      { n: "01", title: "The world before", blocks: [
        "After Cannae, Hannibal stayed 13 years in Italy, undefeated in great battles, but unable to bend Rome. Meanwhile, the war was changing elsewhere. In Hispania, a young Roman commander, Publius Cornelius Scipio, took New Carthage in 209 BC and won at Ilipa in 206 BC, driving Carthage out of the peninsula.",
        "Elected consul for 205 BC, Scipio proposed taking the war to Africa, to force Carthage to call Hannibal back. The Senate, suspicious, gave him Sicily and permission to cross if he thought it good. He trained the army for a year. The core was two legions of survivors of Cannae, sent to Sicily as a punishment.",
        { timeline: [
          ["206 BC", "Scipio wins at Ilipa. Masinissa, a Numidian prince, trades Carthage for Rome."],
          ["204 BC", "Scipio lands in Africa, near Utica."],
          ["203 BC", "He sets fire at night to the enemy camps near Utica and wins at the Great Plains. The Numidian king Syphax is captured."],
          ["203 BC", "Carthage negotiates peace and calls Hannibal back from Italy."],
          ["202 BC", "Carthage breaks the truce. Hannibal gathers his army at Hadrumetum."],
          ["October 202 BC", "Battle near Zama."]
        ]}
      ]},
      { n: "02", title: "Main figures", blocks: [
        { h: "Scipio, 33 years old" },
        "Son of a consul killed in Hispania, he had fought at the Ticinus and escaped from Cannae. He studied Hannibal like no one else in Rome, and copied from him what worked: the strong cavalry, the maneuver on the ends, the flexibility of the line. At Zama, he used the Roman army in a different way, leaving corridors and changing the formation in the middle of the fight.",
        { h: "Hannibal, 45 years old" },
        "He came back to Africa with 15,000 to 20,000 veterans from Italy, to a land he had not seen since he was nine. This time he did not have the advantage he had always had: he lacked cavalry, and a good part of his army were recruits and mercenaries gathered in a hurry.",
        { h: "The other names" },
        "Masinissa, a Numidian prince who had fought for Carthage in Hispania, now fought for Rome, with an eye on the throne of Syphax. He brought about 4,000 horsemen and 6,000 foot soldiers. Laelius, Scipio's closest friend, commanded the Roman cavalry.",
        { note: "The meeting: according to Polybius and Livy, on the eve the two generals met alone, with interpreters, between the armies. Hannibal offered peace and Scipio refused. The words that the authors put in their mouths are literary invention." }
      ]},
      { n: "03", title: "Step by step", blocks: [
        { h: "The forces" },
        "Scipio had about 29,000 to 30,000 men, with some 6,000 horsemen, most of them Masinissa's [[Numidians|númidas]]. Hannibal had about 40,000, perhaps more, with 80 elephants and only some 4,000 horsemen. For the first time, the better cavalry was on the Roman side.",
        { h: "The terrain" },
        "A plain in the interior of present-day Tunisia. The exact place is not known; the most accepted candidate is the plain of Draa el Metnan, south of ancient Sicca, today El Kef. 'Zama' is the name the Roman authors gave, after a nearby city.",
        { formation: true },
        { h: "The formation" },
        "Hannibal put the 80 elephants and the light infantry in front. Behind them, three lines: first the mercenaries, Gauls, Ligurians, Moors and Balearics; then the Carthaginians and Africans recruited on the spot; and last, some 200 meters behind, his veterans from Italy, the reserve. The Carthaginian cavalry stood on the right; the Numidian, on the left.",
        "Scipio changed the arrangement of the [[legion|legião]]. Instead of the usual checkerboard, he placed the [[maniples|manípulo]] of the [[principes]] exactly behind the [[hastati]], opening straight corridors from front to back. He filled the corridors with [[velites|vélites]]. Laelius stood on the left, and Masinissa on the right.",
        { h: "1. The elephants" },
        "The battle began with cavalry skirmishes. Then Hannibal released the elephants. The Romans sounded trumpets and beat their weapons on their shields, and many elephants panicked. On the Carthaginian left, several turned and trampled their own Numidian cavalry, and Masinissa took the chance to attack it. The other elephants ran into the corridors, where the velites, who fell back through them, attacked them with javelins. Some did damage among the hastati before being driven off.",
        { h: "2. The cavalries leave the field" },
        "On the Carthaginian right, some elephants disrupted the cavalry, and Laelius attacked. On both ends, Hannibal's cavalry fled, with the Roman and the Numidian behind it. For a good while, the cavalries vanished from the battle.",
        { h: "3. The first and the second line" },
        "The hastati faced the mercenaries and pushed them back. The second Carthaginian line did not let the mercenaries pass, and they even fought against their own comrades. The principes went in to help, and the second line was broken too. The survivors of both fled to the sides. Hannibal did not order the veterans to go in: they lowered their spears to stop the fugitives from breaking their line.",
        { h: "4. The pause" },
        "Then something rare in an ancient battle happened. Scipio sounded the retreat, gathered the hastati who were pursuing the enemy and reorganized the army in a single wide line: hastati in the center, principes and [[triarii]] on the ends. The ground between the armies was slippery with blood and full of bodies. On the other side, Hannibal gathered the survivors on the ends of the line of veterans.",
        { h: "5. The final clash" },
        "The two lines hurled themselves at each other with, in the words of Polybius, 'the greatest fire and fury'. They were similar in number and in courage, and the fight stood still for a long time. It was decided by those who arrived from outside: Laelius and Masinissa came back from the pursuit and attacked the rear of Hannibal's veterans. Most died where they stood."
      ]},
      { n: "04", title: "Legacy", blocks: [
        { h: "Result" },
        "According to Polybius, Carthage lost 20,000 dead and 20,000 captured, and the Romans about 1,500 dead. Hannibal fled to Hadrumetum and told the Senate of Carthage that the only way out was to ask for peace. It was his only defeat in a great pitched battle.",
        { h: "The peace" },
        "The terms, accepted in 201 BC, were harsh: 10,000 talents of silver over 50 years, hostages, surrender of the elephants, a fleet of only 10 warships, the loss of the territories outside Africa and a ban on making war without Rome's permission. Masinissa became king of Numidia. Scipio earned a triumph and the nickname 'Africanus'.",
        { h: "Why it matters" },
        "Rome became the master of the western Mediterranean, and within 50 years would dominate the eastern one too. Carthage was never again a power; it was destroyed by Rome in 146 BC. Zama also showed that the Roman army had learned: the rigid line that died at Cannae had become a flexible machine, which changed formation in the middle of the battle.",
        { h: "Curiosities and controversies" },
        { note: "The name: the ancients speak of Zama, but it is not clear which of the several cities with that name, and Polybius places the battle near a place called Margaron. The name 'Zama' stuck by tradition." },
        { note: "The end of Hannibal: after the war, he governed Carthage, was forced into exile and served kings who were enemies of Rome. Around 183 BC, surrounded by Roman agents in Bithynia, he took poison. Scipio died in the same period, kept away from Rome." }
      ]}
    ]
  }
});

